{
  "dom_id": "step-q183553",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "If ${n}^{2}$ is odd, then $n$ is odd.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language, given that $n$ is a natural number.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's denote:\n\n- $P(n):$ ${n}^{2}$ is odd\n\n- $Q(n):$ $n$ is odd\n\nThen, the given sentence has the following logical structure:\n\n$$\nP(n)⇒Q(n)\n$$\n\nThis, in turn, is equivalent to \n\n$$\n∀\\,n\\in U,\\,P(n)⇒Q(n)\n$$\n\nwhere the universal set $U$ is usually apparent from the context. In this case, $n\\in N.$\n\nTherefore, using formal language, our statement reads \n\nFor all natural number $n,$ if ${n}^{2}$ is odd, then $n$ is odd.\n\nOr, more formally:\n\n$∀\\,n\\in N,\\,$ (${n}^{2}$ is odd) $⇒$ ($n$ is odd)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
